A read-only label validator that accepts both `String` and string literals (`&str`) can be designed using a trait to abstract the input type while ensuring only valid UTF-8 string content is accepted. The validator operates on a trait object that implements `AsRef<str>`, allowing both owned `String` and borrowed `&str` to be passed without ownership transfer.

```rust
use std::fmt;

pub trait LabelValidator: fmt::Display {
    fn validate(&self) -> Result<(), String>;
}

impl LabelValidator for String {
    fn validate(&self) -> Result<(), String> {
        if self.is_empty() {
            return Err("Label cannot be empty".to_string());
        }
        if self.chars().any(|c| c.is_ascii_control()) {
            return Err("Label contains control characters".to_string());
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl LabelValidator for &str {
    fn validate(&self) -> Result<(), String> {
        if self.is_empty() {
            return Err("Label cannot be empty".to_string());
        }
        if self.chars().any(|c| c.is_ascii_control()) {
            return Err("Label contains control characters".to_string());
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

// Example usage
fn main() {
    let label1: String = "Hello".to_string();
    let label2: &str = "World";

    match LabelValidator::validate(&label1) {
        Ok(_) => println!("Label1 is valid"),
        Err(e) => println!("Label1 invalid: {}", e),
    }

    match LabelValidator::validate(label2) {
        Ok(_) => println!("Label2 is valid"),
        Err(e) &label2) {
        Err(e) => println!("Label2 invalid: {}", e),
    }
}
```

Note: The above example demonstrates a trait-based design with `AsRef<str>` compatibility. In practice, the trait should be implemented for `AsRef<str>` to accept both `String` and `&str`

